Task: codex-mark round 4 — P0=1 P1=1 P2=2 -> DENIED in substance (HELD by the cap); all four fixed
Action: Round 4 found a P0 in MY round-3 fix. `release` never cleared `held`, and an exiting signal handler also fires the EXIT trap, so INT/TERM called release twice with held=1 -> two rmdir calls. Reproduced in both /bin/sh and /bin/bash, then reproduced the harm: a successor that acquires the lock between the two calls has its lock deleted by the second. That is worse than the unserialized-marking bug round 3 fixed. Fixed by clearing `held` before the rmdir — which is exactly what codex-run.py's Lock.release already does, and which round 3 cited as precedent while failing to copy the one line that makes it work. Also: both round-3 regression pins were hollow. The "lock released on the fail path" test used an incomplete log, which fails at the sidecar check BEFORE mkdir, so it asserted about a lock never taken; retargeted at a missing-verdict failure and now also asserts the error message to prove execution reached past the lock. The signal pin only grepped source; replaced with a probe that EXTRACTS the real lock block from the shipped script, shadows rmdir to count calls, sends a real TERM, and asserts rc=143 and exactly one rmdir. Both new assertions regression-checked against their respective broken versions. Corrected two false doc claims of my own: the O_EXCL comment (codex-run unlinks a stale log first, so it does not "refuse to start") and the remaining inode-binding text in CHANGELOG and the rulebook README.
