[2026-08-21T00:00:00Z]
Task: codex-mark — delete the lock, snapshot the transcript instead (maintainer chose this over a 5th round)
Action: Rounds 3 and 4 both found defects in the LOCK, never in the thing the lock guarded. Circuit breaker: two findings in one class means stop patching and question the design. The lock's only job was closing the window between the digest check and the verdict parse, because the old code read $log twice. Reading it ONCE into a mktemp snapshot closes that window with no lock, no ownership, no signal handlers, and no release — the whole defect class stops existing. codex-mark now only CHECKS for codex-run's lock (a `[ -d ]` test) and refuses if present; it never acquires it. Cleanup is a bare EXIT trap doing `rm -f` on our own temp file, which is safe to run twice, so INT/TERM keep their default disposition. Net: 46 lines of lock machinery plus 37 in tests removed, replaced by ~10.
