Action: Round 5 confirmed all eight properties of the lock-free design. Property 3 is the one that mattered: with a concurrent codex-run writing at every step, no interleaving produces a marker that should not exist — acceptance requires the exact bytes the completion record hashed. Marked PASS at eca9953, rounds=5. Then, because a 6th round was being run anyway for the flaky test, folded in the rest instead of logging them: DELETED the temp-file assertion (it counted every tmp* in the SHARED temp dir, so an unrelated process failed it once during the review itself, and its `AFTER <= BEFORE` shape meant an unrelated deletion would hide a real leak — a flaky weak pin guarding a one-line EXIT trap is worth less than no pin); strengthened the read-once pin to assert HEADCALLS -eq 1 literally rather than inferring it from the verdict; corrected the signal comment, which claimed the worst outcome was a leftover temp file when a signal can also land after the round write, either .prev move, or the marker itself; added the .complete sidecar to the README state-file inventory; removed a duplicated .prev sentence I had introduced.
